## Isip, Kilos-Loob & Human Dignity
@ Isip | Kilos-loob | Human dignity | Responsibility
Which human capacity is associated with understanding and judgment?|0|Isip concerns knowing and reasoning.
Which capacity is associated with choosing and willing action?|1|Kilos-loob concerns deliberate choice.
What names the inherent worth of every human person?|2|Dignity does not depend on wealth or rank.
What connects a deliberate action with answerability for its consequences?|3|Choices carry responsibility.
@ Verify information | Consider consequences | Respect equal worth | Accept accountability
A pupil hears an accusation before judging. What should come first?|0|Judgment needs a factual basis.
A decision may affect several classmates. What should be examined?|1|Actions have effects on others.
A poor pupil is denied ordinary courtesy. What principle should guide correction?|2|Social status does not remove dignity.
A chosen action causes avoidable harm. What is appropriate?|3|Acknowledgment supports repair.
@ Prudence | Justice | Compassion | Integrity
A person weighs evidence before deciding. Which value?|0|Prudence involves careful judgment.
A rule is applied fairly to comparable cases. Which value?|1|Justice concerns fair treatment.
Someone responds sensitively to another's suffering. Which value?|2|Compassion recognizes another's need.
A person keeps an honest commitment when unwatched. Which value?|3|Integrity aligns conduct and principle.
@ Deliberate choice | Coercion | Ignorance of a relevant fact | Reflection
A learner understands alternatives and selects an action freely. Which condition?|0|The choice is informed and intentional.
A threat severely restricts available action. Which condition?|1|Pressure can constrain voluntariness.
A decision-maker lacks essential information. Which limitation?|2|Incomplete knowledge affects judgment.
A person reviews motives after an action. What process?|3|Reflection examines one's conduct.
@ Dignity is not earned by performance | Freedom includes responsibility | Facts and motives both matter | Repair should address actual harm
Why does academic failure not remove a pupil's worth?|0|Human worth is not a test score.
Why is 'I chose it' insufficient to justify harming others?|1|Choice is subject to ethical evaluation.
Why should judging an action examine what happened and what was intended?|2|Outcome alone does not explain every relevant feature.
Why is an apology incomplete when avoidable damage is left unaddressed?|3|Responsible correction includes appropriate restitution.
## Family & Relationships
@ Communication | Respect | Cooperation | Boundaries
Sharing needs clearly and listening carefully develops what?|0|Mutual understanding needs communication.
Recognizing another's worth during disagreement shows what?|1|Disagreement need not remove respect.
Sharing a household task toward a common goal demonstrates what?|2|Cooperation combines contributions.
Agreed limits on private information establish what?|3|Boundaries protect appropriate personal space.
@ Active listening | Assertive expression | Empathy | Negotiation
Restating a family member's concern to check understanding is what?|0|Listening checks the received meaning.
'I need time to finish this task' stated calmly illustrates what?|1|Assertiveness expresses needs respectfully.
Considering how another person may feel illustrates what?|2|Empathy attends to another's experience.
Discussing a workable division of responsibilities is what?|3|Negotiation seeks an acceptable arrangement.
@ Trust | Reliability | Consent | Mutual support
Honesty over time helps build what?|0|Trust develops through credible conduct.
Completing an agreed task consistently shows what?|1|Reliability makes commitments dependable.
Voluntary agreement to share personal information is what?|2|Consent should be informed and freely given.
Helping each other through difficulties illustrates what?|3|Support can be reciprocal.
@ Clarify the misunderstanding | Agree on a fair process | Seek appropriate help | Respect different family arrangements
An argument begins from an ambiguous message. What is useful first?|0|Clarification can address the actual issue.
Two people dispute task allocation. What supports cooperation?|1|A fair process considers needs and ability.
A person faces threats or abuse at home. What is appropriate?|2|Safety requires support rather than silent endurance.
Classmates assume every household has identical roles. What should be corrected?|3|Families differ in structure and circumstances.
@ Reconciliation does not require tolerating abuse | Support differs from control | Fairness may require different contributions | Privacy and safety must both be considered
Why should a harmed person not be pressured to accept ongoing mistreatment?|0|Safety and dignity remain important.
Someone calls constant surveillance caring. What distinction matters?|1|Care should not erase autonomy.
One caregiver has a disability. Why may an equal split of physical tasks be unfair?|2|Capacity and circumstances affect equitable sharing.
A family member shares a credible immediate safety concern. What judgment is needed?|3|Confidentiality must be weighed with appropriate protection.
## Pakikipagkapwa
@ Shared personhood | Empathy | Respect | Solidarity
What is central to kapwa as an interpersonal concept?|0|Others are recognized as fellow persons.
Trying to understand another's feelings expresses what?|1|Empathy attends to another's experience.
Treating a person courteously despite disagreement expresses what?|2|Respect preserves dignity.
Working with others to address a common hardship expresses what?|3|Solidarity joins shared concern and action.
@ Listening | Inclusion | Cooperation | Sensitivity
A quiet classmate is given time to explain. Which practice?|0|Listening allows another voice to be heard.
A group adapts its activity so a disabled member can participate. Which practice?|1|Inclusion removes avoidable barriers.
Members divide a project fairly and help one another. Which practice?|2|Cooperation coordinates effort.
A joke is stopped after noticing it humiliates someone. Which practice?|3|Sensitivity attends to interpersonal effects.
@ Prejudice | Stereotyping | Discrimination | Bullying
A negative judgment precedes knowing an individual. What is this?|0|Prejudice prejudges a person or group.
A fixed trait is assigned to everyone in a group. What is this?|1|Stereotypes erase individual variation.
Someone receives unfair treatment because of group membership. What is this?|2|Discrimination concerns unequal treatment.
Repeated hostile conduct exploits a power imbalance. What concern arises?|3|This is characteristic bullying behavior.
@ Ask rather than assume | Protect dignity | Offer appropriate support | Admit and correct mistakes
A peer's needs are unclear. What is helpful?|0|Asking respects their knowledge of their situation.
A disagreement becomes personal ridicule. What should guide intervention?|1|Critique need not demean a person.
A classmate is struggling but has not requested a specific solution. What is appropriate?|2|Support should fit the person's needs and wishes.
A false rumor was shared. What is responsible?|3|Correction can limit ongoing harm.
@ Kindness need not be agreement | Help should preserve agency | Bystanders have choices | Context does not excuse humiliation
Why can a respectful person reject a friend's proposal?|0|Respect and consent are distinct.
Why should assistance not unnecessarily take over another's decisions?|1|Support can strengthen rather than replace agency.
Why is silence during repeated harassment not the only available response?|2|Safe intervention and reporting can support the target.
A harmful joke is defended as customary. What should be evaluated?|3|Tradition does not erase its effect on dignity.
## Society & the Common Good
@ Common good | Public service | Civic participation | Stewardship
Conditions that enable community members to flourish concern what?|0|The common good includes shared social conditions.
Work performed to serve community needs illustrates what?|1|Public service is oriented toward public benefit.
Residents contribute to a lawful consultation. What is this?|2|Participation gives people a voice.
Responsible care of shared resources illustrates what?|3|Stewardship considers present and future use.
@ Fair access | Transparency | Accountability | Cooperation
A public service is made available without arbitrary exclusion. Which principle?|0|Fair access supports shared benefit.
Decision-makers disclose relevant project information. Which principle?|1|Transparency permits scrutiny.
Officials explain how funds were used. Which principle?|2|Accountability requires answerability.
Neighbors coordinate a clean-up. Which practice?|3|Cooperation combines contributions.
@ Public space | Shared resource | Public health | Environmental quality
A park used by community members is what kind of setting?|0|It supports common activities.
A village water source used by many households is what?|1|Several users depend on it.
Safe sanitation supports which collective concern?|2|Disease prevention has community-wide effects.
Clean air and reduced pollution support what?|3|Environmental conditions affect shared well-being.
@ Include affected people | Compare benefits and burdens | Use evidence | Protect vulnerable groups
Planning a relocation affects residents' lives. Whose views should be included?|0|Affected people hold relevant knowledge and interests.
A project helps one group but imposes costs on another. What must be examined?|1|Distribution matters as well as totals.
Competing flood-control proposals need evaluation. What should guide it?|2|Credible evidence helps assess likely effects.
A service change excludes those with the least alternatives. What deserves attention?|3|Vulnerability can magnify harm.
@ Majority preference is not sufficient alone | Shared benefit can require restraint | Future generations matter | Participation must be meaningful
Why should popular projects still be checked against basic rights?|0|The common good cannot ignore dignity.
Why limit one person's excessive use of a shared water source during shortage?|1|Unrestricted use can harm others' access.
Why consider long-term environmental costs?|2|Today's choices affect later communities.
Why is consultation weak if every decision is final before people speak?|3|A voice should have a genuine opportunity to influence decisions.
## Moral Decision-Making
@ Identify the issue | Gather relevant facts | Consider alternatives | Evaluate consequences
What begins a careful response to an ethical dilemma?|0|The actual moral problem must be clear.
What helps prevent judgment based on rumor?|1|Decisions need reliable information.
What avoids assuming only one action is possible?|2|Alternatives reveal available choices.
What examines who may be helped or harmed?|3|Consequences affect ethical assessment.
@ Intention | Means | Circumstances | Consequences
What describes the purpose behind an action?|0|Intention concerns what is sought.
What describes the method used to pursue a goal?|1|Means concern how a goal is pursued.
What includes contextual pressures and conditions?|2|Circumstances affect understanding of the action.
What describes effects following an action?|3|Consequences are resulting outcomes.
@ Honesty | Fairness | Care | Responsibility
Correcting a misleading statement upholds what?|0|Honesty respects truth.
Applying a justified rule consistently upholds what?|1|Fairness opposes arbitrary favoritism.
Attending to another person's vulnerability expresses what?|2|Care responds to needs and relationships.
Accepting appropriate repair after causing harm expresses what?|3|Responsibility includes answerability.
@ Conflict of values | Peer pressure | Rationalization | Reflective judgment
Loyalty and truthful reporting pull in different directions. What is present?|0|Important commitments can conflict.
A group urges someone to act against their considered judgment. What influence?|1|Peers can pressure behavior.
A person invents excuses after an unethical choice. What process?|2|Rationalization defends a preferred action.
A person tests reasons before selecting a course. What process?|3|Reflection examines justification.
@ A good goal does not justify every method | Consent must be voluntary | Repair should match the harm | Review decisions when facts change
Why is cheating to win a prize for charity still ethically problematic?|0|The chosen method also matters.
Why does agreement under a serious threat require scrutiny?|1|Coercion can undermine free agreement.
Why is saying sorry insufficient after refusing an achievable remedy?|2|Ethical response includes correcting consequences.
A judgment relied on a claim later shown false. What should happen?|3|Sound decisions respond to corrected evidence.
## Personal Development & Self-Awareness
@ Self-awareness | Strength | Area for development | Value
Recognizing one's thoughts and emotional patterns develops what?|0|Self-awareness examines personal experience.
A skill performed effectively is what?|1|Strengths are useful capabilities.
A skill needing further practice is what?|2|Development areas can improve.
An enduring priority guiding choice is what?|3|Values shape what matters.
@ Specific goal | Measurable progress | Feasible plan | Review point
'I will practice reading for 15 minutes daily' improves which goal quality?|0|It states a concrete action.
Counting completed practice sessions supports what?|1|Measurement shows progress.
Matching study demands to available time supports what?|2|Feasibility matters for execution.
Scheduling a weekly check of the plan creates what?|3|Review permits adjustment.
@ Reflection | Feedback | Habit | Self-regulation
Examining what went well after a task is what?|0|Reflection extracts learning from experience.
Receiving a teacher's specific observations provides what?|1|Feedback adds an external perspective.
A behavior becomes regular through repetition. What is forming?|2|Repeated cues and actions can form habits.
Managing attention and action toward a goal is what?|3|Self-regulation connects intention and behavior.
@ Ask for support | Break work into steps | Track patterns | Adjust strategy
A task exceeds current skill. What is constructive?|0|Guidance can support learning.
A large project feels unmanageable. What helps organization?|1|Smaller tasks clarify next actions.
Repeated distraction occurs at similar times. What helps understanding?|2|Records can reveal triggers.
Practice continues without improvement. What should be examined?|3|Method may need change.
@ Self-worth is not one result | Feedback can be selective and useful | Goals should reflect real constraints | Comparison needs context
Why does one low score not establish total inability?|0|One outcome does not define the whole person.
Why need a learner not accept an insulting global label as constructive criticism?|1|Useful feedback concerns specific behavior and evidence.
Why should a plan change when caregiving duties increase?|2|Resources and obligations affect feasibility.
Why can comparing raw scores from different tests mislead self-assessment?|3|Difficulty and conditions may differ.
## Adolescent Development
@ Physical development | Cognitive development | Emotional development | Social development
Changes in height and bodily maturation belong to which domain?|0|They concern the developing body.
Increasing ability to reason about possibilities belongs to which domain?|1|Thinking skills develop.
Learning to recognize and manage feelings belongs to which domain?|2|Emotional understanding develops.
Changing peer and family relationships belong to which domain?|3|Relationships shape social development.
@ Individual variation | Puberty | Identity exploration | Growing autonomy
Why do same-age pupils not mature at identical rates?|0|Developmental timing varies.
What process includes biological changes associated with sexual maturation?|1|Puberty involves hormonal and bodily change.
Examining personal interests and commitments illustrates what?|2|Identity develops through exploration.
Taking increasing responsibility for decisions illustrates what?|3|Autonomy develops with capacity and experience.
@ Reliable information | Supportive relationships | Healthy routines | Appropriate help-seeking
What should guide questions about bodily changes?|0|Accurate information limits misinformation.
What can help an adolescent feel heard during difficulty?|1|Trusted supportive people can help.
Regular rest, nourishment and activity contribute to what?|2|Routines support well-being.
Persistent distress interferes with daily functioning. What response is constructive?|3|Qualified support may be needed.
@ Peer influence | Risk assessment | Personal boundaries | Perspective-taking
A friend's behavior changes a pupil's choices. What influence?|0|Peers can shape decisions.
Comparing possible harm and benefit develops what?|1|Risk assessment supports informed choice.
Communicating what contact or sharing is acceptable establishes what?|2|Boundaries support dignity and consent.
Considering another person's point of view practices what?|3|Perspective-taking supports understanding.
@ Avoid a single maturity timetable | Autonomy and guidance can coexist | Popularity does not establish safety | Respect privacy without ignoring danger
Why should early and late developers not be ranked by worth?|0|Timing does not determine dignity.
Why can a teenager make more choices while still seeking adult guidance?|1|Support need not cancel developing independence.
A dangerous challenge is popular online. What should determine participation?|2|Evidence about risk matters more than popularity.
A friend describes credible immediate danger. What must be considered alongside confidentiality?|3|Appropriate protective help may be necessary.
## Philosophy
@ Metaphysics | Epistemology | Ethics | Logic
Which branch examines fundamental questions about reality?|0|Metaphysics concerns being and reality.
Which branch examines knowledge and justification?|1|Epistemology asks what and how we know.
Which branch examines right action and moral value?|2|Ethics concerns moral evaluation.
Which branch studies principles of valid reasoning?|3|Logic evaluates inferential structure.
@ Premise | Conclusion | Argument | Counterexample
What statement supplies support in reasoning?|0|A premise is offered as a reason.
What statement is the reasoning intended to establish?|1|The conclusion is the supported claim.
What combines supporting statements with an asserted inference?|2|An argument relates reasons and conclusion.
What case can refute a universal claim?|3|One contrary instance challenges universality.
@ Deductive validity | Soundness | Inductive strength | Conceptual clarity
What holds when true premises cannot yield a false conclusion?|0|Validity concerns logical consequence.
What requires a valid argument with true premises?|1|Soundness adds premise truth.
What concerns how strongly evidence makes a conclusion probable?|2|Induction supports degrees of confidence.
Defining ambiguous terms improves what?|3|Clear concepts reduce equivocation.
@ Consequentialism | Deontology | Virtue ethics | Ethics of care
Which ethical approach centrally evaluates outcomes?|0|Consequences are its primary focus.
Which approach emphasizes duties and constraints?|1|Deontology concerns obligations.
Which approach focuses on character and cultivated dispositions?|2|Virtue ethics asks what kind of person to become.
Which approach emphasizes relationships and responsiveness to need?|3|Care ethics foregrounds relational responsibility.
@ Distinguish validity from truth | Question hidden assumptions | Reject personal attacks as proof | Recognize limits of an example
A valid inference begins with a false premise. What distinction matters?|0|Validity alone does not guarantee a true conclusion.
A claim depends on an unstated definition of success. What should be examined?|1|An assumption may control the argument.
A critic attacks a philosopher's appearance instead of their reasons. What response is justified?|2|The attack does not evaluate the reasoning.
One experience is used to prove a claim about all people. What caution applies?|3|The example may not support the universal scope.
## Human Freedom & Responsibility
@ Voluntariness | Deliberation | Constraint | Accountability
An action is chosen without coercion. What feature is relevant?|0|Voluntariness concerns freedom in choosing.
A person weighs alternatives before acting. What process?|1|Deliberation examines available reasons.
A barrier limits realistic options. What condition?|2|Constraints restrict possibilities.
A person answers for a deliberate harmful action. What principle?|3|Responsibility involves accountability.
@ Informed choice | Coercion | Impulsiveness | Reflection
A person understands relevant options and effects before deciding. What quality?|0|Information supports meaningful choice.
A threat forces apparent agreement. What issue?|1|Coercion can compromise consent.
A person acts immediately without considering known risks. What pattern?|2|Impulse can bypass deliberation.
A person reviews the reasons behind a choice. What practice?|3|Reflection evaluates conduct.
@ Personal agency | Respect for others | Social conditions | Moral responsibility
The capacity to initiate purposeful action concerns what?|0|Agency concerns acting intentionally.
Recognizing that one's actions affect other people's freedom invokes what?|1|Others also have claims and boundaries.
Access to education changes available opportunities. What factor?|2|Institutions can expand or restrict choices.
A person deliberately breaks a justified commitment. What issue arises?|3|Their choice can be ethically evaluated.
@ Accept consequences | Make appropriate repair | Seek reliable advice | Consider alternatives
After freely making a choice, what responsibility follows?|0|Chosen actions can have consequences.
A careless act damages borrowed property. What response fits responsibility?|1|Repair addresses the loss.
A decision involves unfamiliar serious risks. What is prudent?|2|Relevant guidance can improve judgment.
A person feels trapped between two harmful choices. What should be explored?|3|Other feasible options may exist.
@ Freedom is not unlimited license | Reduced control can affect responsibility | Different constraints require examination | Intent and effects both matter
Why does freedom not justify deliberately violating another's rights?|0|Other people's freedom and dignity also matter.
Why should severe coercion be considered when judging an action?|1|Voluntariness may be restricted.
Why can two apparently identical choices involve different degrees of freedom?|2|Circumstances can change realistic options.
Why should a good intention not end analysis of a harmful act?|3|The means and consequences also require evaluation.
